                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesAnyonicTopologicalOrderOnTori"} Anyonic topological order on tori
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#further_resources"} Further resources
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %em topological order
            (
            %a{:href => "#Wen89"}> Wen 89
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#WenNiu90"} Wen &amp; Niu 90
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wen91"} Wen 91
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wen93"} 93
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wen95"} 95
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GuWen09"} Gu &amp; Wen 09, p. 2
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ChenGuWen2010"} Chen, Gu &amp; Wen 10
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
            where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"} gapped
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+states"} ground states
            exhibit a “topological” (really:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em homotopical
          %blockquote
            %p
              (“While ostensibly coined in 1989 by Xiao-Gang Wen, the term ‘topological order’ has been in use since 1972 to describe the behavior of the classical xy model. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01765"}> arXiv:2308.01765
              \&amp;rbrack;)
          %p
            For such dependency to be rich (non-abelian), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of gapped ground states should be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            (jargon: “degenerate ground states”).
          %p
            Moreover, for this to qualify as topologically ordered, one typically demands that these degenerate ground states are those of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"}> anyons
            ”. A popular more succinct way of making this somewhat more precise is to say that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            of (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in) a topologically ordered material, when restricted to the energy=0 ground states, is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            equal or akin to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            with
            = succeed "-insertions:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+line"} Wilson line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
          %p
            In short then, topological order is meant to be that aspect of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            which is related to the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"} anyons
            in the material, in one way or other. Via this relation, topological order is closely related to considerations in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum computation
          %h3#ViaDegenerateAnyonicGroundStates Via degenerate anyonic ground states
          %p
            The definition is traditionally a little vague, but the hallmark of a
            %em topological order
            is meant to be the presence, in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GuWen09"} Gu &amp; Wen 09, p. 2
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "parafermion#Tsvelik14b"} Tsvelik 2014b
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                degenerate
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
                (
                %a{:href => "#Wen89"}> Wen 89, p. 4
                ,
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a{:href => "#Wen95"} Wen 95, Sec. 1.1
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
                excitations/defects whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
                constitute
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representations"} braid group representations
                (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon+statistics"}> fractional statistics
                ”).
          %p
            The original articles (
            %a{:href => "#Wen89"}> Wen 89
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#WenNiu90"} Wen &amp; Niu 90
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wen91"} Wen 91
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wen93"} 93
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Wen95"} 95
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phases
            with distinct ground state degeneracy exhibit distinct topological order. The demand that also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
            should be non-abelian for there to be a topological order seems to appear first in
            %a{:href => "#Wen91Review"} Wen 91 review
            (then also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#GuWen09"} Gu &amp; Wen 09, p. 2
          %p
          #RelationBetweenDefiningConditions.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (relation between these conditions)
              %br/
              By “degenerate ground state” one just means that the sub-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
              (for crystalline materials these are the quantum states of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
              in the crystal lattice of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} atomic nuclei
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %p#RelationToSRE
              (It has been proposed that the presence of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short-range+entanglement"}> short-range entanglement
              ” in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
              implies that the ground state Hilbert space is 1-dimensional. In this sense, the condition that the ground state be degenerate implies that there is
              %em no
              “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short-range+entanglement"}> short-range entanglement
              ” if there is “topological order”.)
            %p
              Since (by
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch-Floquet+theory"} Bloch-Floquet theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+sections"} space of sections
              of the Bloch bundle, and since (by assumption of a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sub-bundle"} sub-bundle
              which is called the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank+of+a+vector+bundle"} rank
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %p
              (In making these statement there is some tacit switching between single-electron theory and its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
              involved, sorting out of which would be necessary for being more precise about these matters.)
            %p
              But since the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+vector+bundle"} connection
              on the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
              (namely in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} U(n)
              for
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %p
              Similarly, the presence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"} anyons
              broadly means that there are families of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic deformations
              of the material which have the effect of transforming the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
              forming a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representation"} braid group representation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %p
              However, there are also non-trivial “abelian anyons”, namely braid group representations which are just complex 1-dimensional, and – even if not as interesting as their non-abelian cousins – these are counted as perfectly valid instances of the concept of anyons (in fact it was first a struggle and then a breakthrough to detect abelian anyons in
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+categories"} fusion categories
              to speak about anyons, require of a “topological order” only that it contains anyons and possibly just abelian anyons, in which case it is maybe not so clear whether the ground state needs to be degenerate and/or the Berry connection be non-abelian.
            %p
          %h3#ViaTopologicalEntanglementEntropy Via topological entanglement entropy
          %p
            It is now thought that some/all of the
            %a{:href => "#ViaDegenerateAnyonicGroundStates"} above
            characteristics may be captured
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theoretically
            in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement+entropy"} entanglement entropy
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
          %p
            The non-degeneracy of the ground state is related to
            %em absence
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short-range+entanglement"}> short-range entanglement
            ”; and the existence of anyon excitations is related to the
            %em presence
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long-range+entanglement"}> long-range entanglement
            ” (
            %a{:href => "#ChenGuWen2010"}> Chen, Gu &amp; Wen 10, Sec. 5
            ) witnessed by a non-vanishing
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+entanglement+entropy"} topological entanglement entropy
            (
            %a{:href => "entanglement+entropy#KitaevPreskill06"}> Kitaev &amp; Preskill 2006
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "entanglement+entropy#LevinWen06"} Levin &amp; Wen 2006
          %p
            Often one sees topological order being related also to (1)
            %em strong interaction
            and/or (2)
            %em strong quantum correlation
            (
            %a{:href => "#Wen91Review"}> Wen 91 review
            ) between the electrons (while the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron+band+theory"} electron band theory
            that gives rise to the widely-accepted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
            assumes that it is sensible to
            %em neglect
            the interaction of electrons between each other and only retain their interaction to an effective Coulomb
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
          %p#BewareOfCorrelation
            Beware that the use of the term “correlation” in the context of topological order (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Wen91Review"} Wen 91 review
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} quantum entanglement
            (cf.
            %a{:href => "#ZCZW19"} ZCZW 19, §1.5
            and generally
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "entanglement#LuoLuo03"} Luo &amp; Luo 03, p. 3
            %em classical correlation
            is indicative of
            %em non-topological
            = succeed "-phases" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau+theory"} Landau theory
          %p Together with the entanglement-theoretic characterization just mentioned, the logic here seems to be the following sequence of schematic implications:
          %svg{:height => "76.247pt", :version => "1.1", :viewbox => "0 0 702.942 76.247", :width => "702.942pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
              %g
                %symbol{:id => "VeUlg9egZzNcMfqibFZTX1rSKp8=-glyph0-0", :overflow => "visible"}
                  %path{:d => "", :style => "stroke:none;"}
