              %path{:d => "M -1.551388 3.067292 C -0.797481 1.446199 1.225956 0.05948 2.386112 0.00088625 C 1.225956 -0.0577075 -0.797481 -1.448333 -1.551388 -3.069426 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(-1,0,0,1,622.1283,46.81552)"}
              %path{:d => "M -1.552587 3.069426 C -0.798681 1.448333 1.228663 0.0577075 2.384913 -0.00088625 C 1.228663 -0.05948 -0.798681 -1.446199 -1.552587 -3.067292 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(1,0,0,-1,628.4354,46.81552)"}
          %blockquote
            %p
              (diagram adapted from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#SatiSchreiber22"} SS22
          %p
            The first steps in this sequence is intuitively plausible and widely expected to hold (eg.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "entanglement+entropy#LuVijay22"} Lu &amp; Viyah 2022, p. 1
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "entanglement+entropy#ZaanenLiuSunSchalm15"} Zaanen, Liu, Sun &amp; Schalm 2015, p. 527
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} Coulomb
            interaction between the electrons – which by itself is certainly strong and long-range – cannot be neglected (hence if the averaging- or screening-effects that make
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron+band+theory"} electron band theory
            work do not apply) then this strong interaction makes the electrons in the ground state be correlated with each other, one way or other, across non-negligible distances; and quantum mechanically this leads to the ground state’s entanglement entropy having long-range contributions, which, essentially by definition, means that it has a constant contribution by the
            %em topological
            entanglement entropy.
          %h2#DefinitionAndExamples A Definition and its Examples
          %p
            A satisfactory definition of “topological order” is not readily manifest from traditional literature. For example, the presence of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"}> anyons
            ” understood as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"} solitons
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            (and that distinction itself is typically not properly made in the literature) localized in 2D space (as for
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+quantum+Hall+systems"} fractional quantum Hall systems
            %em example
            of topological order, but the actual definition should not presuppose this specific incarnation of the general phenomenon. Instead, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} quantum adiabatic theorem
          %p
            We consider here the following more formal and more general Definition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ADefinition"}
            (following
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Topological+Quantum+Gates+in+Homotopy+Type+Theory"} MySS24 §3
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Crystalline+Chern+Order"} SS25
          %p
          #ADefinition.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (topological order)
            %p
              We shall say that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
              exhibits (
              %em> proper
              )
              %em topological order
              if the following three conditions are satisfied:
            %p
              %strong (1. – holonomy)
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_p
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"} gapped
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+states"} ground states
              of the system
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type"} depends
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+function"} differentiably
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
              external parameters
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              varying in some parameter
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %p
              By the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} quantum adiabatic theorem
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paths"} paths
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              induces (asymptotically)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+transformations"} unitary transformations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\gamma
              of the quantum system:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 30px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumAdiabaticTransport.png", :width => "180px"}/
            %p
              %strong (2. – homotopy)
              This dependency on parameter paths is “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+physics+--+contents"}> topological
              ” (which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              jargon for what in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
              really is:
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopica
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \colon \gamma \Rightarrow \gamma'
              of parameter paths (
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
              , keeping their endpoints fixed) does not affect the induced
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+transformations"} unitary transformations
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 30px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumHomotopyInvariance-250907.png", :width => "190px"}/
            %p
              %strong (3. – monodromy)
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              (of any
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is non-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial
              (properly:
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
              of parameter paths with the same endpoints do
              %em not admit
              (#) continuous deformations into each other:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 30px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/NondegenerateQuantumHolonomy.png", :width => "190px"}/
            %p
              and the induced unitary representation of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 30px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumMonodromy-250907.png", :width => "210px"}/
            %p
              is non-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+representation"} trivial
              (properly:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} contains
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreps"} irreps
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 1
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (interpretation)
            %p
              Physically, the 1st condition in Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ADefinition"}
              says that tuning of parameters induces transformations on gapped quantum ground states like
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "adiabatic+quantum+computation#ReferencesGeometricPhaseGates"} holomonic quantum gates
              = succeed ")" do
                %em homotopical
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computing"} topological quantum gates
              on the system.
            %p
              In mathematical jargon, the 1st condition makes the Hilbert spaces form a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} bundle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              whose
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
              are the unitaries
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \mapsto U_\gamma
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+system"} local system
            %p
          %p Now first to see how this general definition reduces to the traditional case:
          %p
          #TraditionalAnyonicTopologicalOrder.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (traditional anyon braiding)
              For cases like plain
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQH+systems"} FQH systems
              on a surface
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset \Sigma^2
              of (quasi-hole/vortex) positions, known as the surface’s
              = succeed ":" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
            #eq:ConfigurationSpaceOfPoints.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≡
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Conf
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} }
                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P
                    \\,\equiv\,
                    \\mathrm{Conf}\big(\Sigma^2\big)
                    \\,\coloneqq\,
                    \\Big\{
                    S \subset \Sigma^2
                    \\,\big\vert\,
                    S \in FinSet
                    \\Big\}
                    \\mathrlap{\,.}
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groups"} fundamental groups
              of the configuration space
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConfigurationSpaceOfPoints"} (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              vortices, are the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface+braid+groups"} surface braid groups
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Br
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≡
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Conf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{Br}_n(\Sigma^2)
                    \\,\equiv\,
                    \\pi_1\big(
                    \\mathrm{Conf}(\Sigma^2)
                    ,
                    n
                    \\big)
                    \\mathrlap{\,,}
            %p
              and their linear representations are the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+statistics"} braid group statistics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyonic
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
            %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 30px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BraidTransport.png", :width => "250px"}/
            %p
              The theoretical identification of
              %a{:href => "quantum+Hall+effect#BraidingPhase"} FQH anyons
              via the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} quantum adiabatic theorem
              applied to this situation goes back to
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "quantum+Hall+effect#ArovasSchriefferWilczek84"} Arovas, Schrieffer &amp; Wilczek 1984
              %a{:href => "quantum+Hall+effect#Su86"} Su 1986
              and this is how (FQH) anyons have been conceptualized ever since, in particular in the literature on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computing"} topological quantum computing
              (see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "quantum+adiabatic+theorem#ReferencesForTopologicalQuantumComputing"} there
            %p
              Incidentally,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+bundles"} flat bundles
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+spaces"} finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces
              over the parameter space
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConfigurationSpaceOfPoints"} (1)
              famously (but not exclusively) arise as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov+connections"} Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov connections
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} bundles
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
              are often known (somewhat undescriptively) as
              = succeed "." do
                %em monodromy representations
          %p
            The phenomenology of the following example reproduces traditional lore about topological order often interpreted in terms of anyons, but it does not involve anyon position parameters. We suggest that the way we obtain this now as another special case of Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalOrder"}
            speaks to the untility of this definition.
          %p
          #TraditionalTopologicalOrderOverTorus.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (traditional topological order over the torus)
            %p
              The phenomenology of the following example reproduces traditional lore about topological order often interpreted in terms of anyons — but it does not involve any anyon position parameters. We suggest that the way we obtain this now as another special case of Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ADefinition"}
              speaks to the relevance of this definition.
            %p
              Namely, it is commonly expected (cf.
              %a{:href => "quantum+Hall+effect#Tong16"} Tong 2016
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Tong-QuantumHallEffect.pdf#page=168"} (5.28)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ≡
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^2 \equiv T^2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              subject to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutation
              relation
            #eq:TopologicalTorusObservables.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ζ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \, B
                    \=
                    \\zeta^2
                    \\,
                    B A
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ζ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta \in \mathbb{C}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root+of+unity"} root of unity
              identified with the FQH anyon
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "quantum+Hall+effect#BraidingPhase"} braiding phase
            %p
              This topological observable algebra
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TopologicalTorusObservables"} (2)
              is traditionally argued by appeal to an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+Chern-Simons+theory"} abelian Chern-Simons field theory
              thought to describe the large-scale (“infrared”) dynamics of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQH+system"} FQH system
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQAH+systems"} FQAH systems
              %em momentum space
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            %p So we are to ask:
            %blockquote
              %p
                %em
                  Does the relation
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TopologicalTorusObservables"} (2)
                  represent the fundamental group of some parameter space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                  associated with FQH systems?
            %p
              Remarkably — the answer is
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/FQH+Anyons"} SS25 §3.3-4
            %p
              Namely, the parameter space of ordinary topological magnetic flux through a surface
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^2
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac flux quantization condition
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} space of maps
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            #eq:MaxwellClassifyingSpace.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{C}P^\infty \simeq B \mathrm{U}(1)
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{U}(1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+theory"} Maxwell
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            #eq:ConfigurationsOfMaxwellFields.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Map
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P
                    \\equiv
                    \\mathrm{Map}\big(
                    \\Sigma^2
                    ,
                    B \mathrm{U}(1)
                    \\big)
                    \\,.
            %p
              However, this has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Map
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_1
                    \\,
                    \\mathrm{Map}\big(
                    T^2
                    ,
                    B \mathrm{U}(1)
                    \\big)
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\mathbb{Z}^2
                    \\mathrlap{\,,}
            %p
              whose representations are given by the algebras of Maxwell Wilson loop observables
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A B
                    \\;=\;
                    B A
                    \\,.
            %p
              On the other hand,
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConfigurationsOfMaxwellFields"} (4)
              describes, of course, only the free magnetic field and ignores its interaction with a 2D electron gas that we are concerned with here. Instead, the surplus magnetic flux quanta on a backdrop of a fractional filling fraction making an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQH+system"} FQH system
              are associated with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
              in a strongly-coupled electron system and as such of exotic nature. To reflect this in their configuration topology we may ask for a deformation of the classical classifying space
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MaxwellClassifyingSpace"} (3)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} defneq
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℂ
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty \defneq \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \mathbb{C}P^n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{C}P^1 \simeq S^2
                    \\,,
            %p leading to an exotic topological flux parameter space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≡
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Map
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P
                    \\;\equiv\;
                    \\mathrm{Map}\big(
                    \\Sigma^2
                    ,
                    S^2
                    \\big)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Remarkably, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of this space is the nonabelian
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer+Heisenberg+group"} integer Heisenberg group
              at level 2 (see
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "cohomotopy#CohomotopyModuliOfSurfaces"} here
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Map
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ^
                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_1
                    \\,
                    \\mathrm{Map}\big(
                    \\Sigma^2
                    ,
                    S^2
                    \\big)
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\widehat{\mathbb{Z}^2}
                    \\mathrlap{\,,}
            %p
              whose representations are exactly of the form
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TopologicalTorusObservables"} (2)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Map
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %munder
                      %mi ζ
                      %mi central
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ]
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} /
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ζ
                      %mn 2
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{C}\Big[
                    \\pi_1
                    \\,
                    \\mathrm{Map}\big(
                    \\Sigma^2
                    ,
                    S^2
                    \\big)
                    \\Big]
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\mathbb{Z}\big[
                    A, B , \underset{central}{\zeta}
                    \\big]\Big/
                    \\Big(
                    A B = \zeta^2 B A
                    \\Big)
                    \\mathrlap{\,.}
            %p
